To answer questions based on visual inputs in Python, we'll first need to use the `object_detector` tool to detect objects in the image. This tool will help us identify and count the number of objects in the image.

<command>
from object_detector import Object_Detector_Tool

# Generate a command using the tool class
command = "detected_objects, object_number = Object_Detector_Tool.execute(image='path/to/image.png', labels=[\"baseball\", \"basket\"], save_object=True, saved_image_path='detected_objects')"
</command>

This command will detect the objects (baseball and basket) and also count the total number of each object detected. If you want to save the detected objects with empty padding, set `save_object=True`. If not, you can simply set `save_object=False`.

To use this tool, replace `'path/to/image.png'` with the actual path to the image file.

After running this tool, the result will be a dictionary similar to the one provided in the previous code snippet. Now, you can use the `Object_Detector_Tool` class from the `object_detector` module to read the result as shown in the command. The output includes a dictionary where keys are the object names and the values are lists of detected objects for each object. The other tools (text_detector, letter_detector, and pixel_depth_estimator) can be used similarly, depending on the task you're trying to solve with the visual inputs.
